{
  "source": "Kinetics-GEBD (Shou et al.), official raw Kinetics-400 validation annotations, CC BY-NC 4.0. Each original annotation range becomes its midpoint. Sorted only for one-to-one matching. The authors’ processed release merges nearby changes; this study keeps individual marks.",
  "annotations": "https://github.com/StanLei52/GEBD",
  "raw_sha256": "2ecd5a1f8366b6d86dec56a39d95db96b2adc9fb9badc6d9a26615d5bef92a3b",
  "recordings": "https://github.com/cvdfoundation/kinetics-dataset",
  "source_revision": "12b1109",
  "model": "gpt-6.1-terra via signed-in local Codex CLI",
  "selection": "Complete matching MP4 members of the first 50331648 compressed bytes from Kinetics-400 validation parts 4 and 10. Require five GEBD observers, matching file stem and video frame count, at least nine seconds, video duration within one frame plus 1 ms of the annotation, video start zero and audio start within 0.1 s of zero. Sort by SHA-256 of reel-motion-holdout-02/ followed by the stem. Take the first eligible film in each category until 12, excluding the 24 original categories. No reader answers used in selection.",
  "conditions": "The model reported its marks after each consecutive two-second interval, before seeing the next one. Four intervals from the start. It saw the picture and evidence derived from recorded channels, without a native WAV. Humans originally annotated the whole film. One model session per film.",
  "prompt": "Watch the activity as it unfolds. Mark the times when one meaningful part of the main activity ends and another begins, one level below the overall activity. Include a change of shot. An ongoing activity may continue across many pictures. It is fine to mark several changes in an interval or none. Use advance with consecutive turn numbers starting at 1. At each call submit your changes in the preceding interval in seconds from the start of the film. Finish when the server returns end_of_view.",
  "scoring": "Fixed scoring clock [0.3,8) s. Each GPT list is compared with all five human lists, each human with the other four. One-to-one temporal matches within 0.5s, F1=2*matches/(marks+reference marks); two empty lists score 1. Equal weight per pair inside film and per film. Human observers annotated the full original clip with their annotation interface; readers see the eight-second prefix, with online decisions irrevocable. The study does not document human audio playback settings. Rotated control preserves within-film relative marks and count on the circular scoring interval. Intervals resample films (10,000 draws). Observers received visual examples and a 100-video qualification exam. GPT received a written request. Each condition has one scored run per film. The film bootstrap does not describe within-reader rerun variability. This is a study-specific all-rater metric, different from the published best-rater benchmark.",
